0113: bus-buffer and AO-gating writes, each judged by CM3_CFG

0112 settled the readback question: the zeros are real, so the block is
either unpowered or being silenced by configuration. 0110 section 8
names the two registers the vendor driver writes before it ever touches
the CM3, and both are cheap to be wrong about. Each candidate writes one
register, reads it back, then re-reads CM3_CFG: a value we never wrote is
the only proof the read path is alive, because a register that echoes our
own write proves no more than an interconnect ack. The read-only AO
delay/hyst pair and sixteen samples of the AO interrupt status then
separate "AO powered, CM3 dead" from "nothing powered", and eight closing
samples keep a "still zero" verdict above the doubt that overturned 0108.

Prediction if c1 or c2 works: CM3_CFG returns a plausible reset value
(0x4052-shaped), not 0xffffffff, and the next boot runs 0110 section 3.
All four silent: the answer is the rail vote, not more register poking.
---
diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_touch.c b/bsd/dev/sweet_touch.c
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_touch.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_touch.c
@@ -1196,6 +1196,8 @@ out:
 #define SWEET_GMU_AO_AHB_FENCE_CTRL        0x24c40u
 #define SWEET_GMU_AHB_FENCE_STATUS         0x24c4cu
 #define SWEET_GMU_CGC_MODE_CNTL            0x24c24u
+#define SWEET_GMU_CGC_DELAY_CNTL           0x24c28u
+#define SWEET_GMU_CGC_HYST_CNTL            0x24c2cu
 #define SWEET_GMU_HFI_CTRL_STATUS          0x14600u
 #define SWEET_GMU_HFI_VERSION_INFO         0x14604u
 #define SWEET_GMU_HFI_QTBL_ADDR            0x14614u
@@ -1209,6 +1211,7 @@ out:
 #define SWEET_GMU_CM3_BOOT_CONFIG          0x14004u
 #define SWEET_GMU_BOOT_KMD_LM_HANDSHAKE    0x147c0u
 #define SWEET_GMU_ICACHE_CONFIG            0x13000u
+#define SWEET_GMU_SYS_BUS_CONFIG           0x1303cu
 #define SWEET_GMU_RPMH_CTRL                0x143a0u
 #define SWEET_GMU_CM3_DTCM_START           0x07000u
 #define SWEET_GMU_PWR_COL_CP_RESP          0x14404u
@@ -1462,6 +1465,66 @@ sweet_gpu_probe(void)
                     sweet_gpu_rd(cc, off + 0x18u), sweet_gpu_rd(cc, off + 0x1cu));
         }
         printf("SWEET GPU: stage8 done\n");
+        if (level < 9) {
+                return;
+        }
+
+        /* Stage 9: 0112 showed the zeros are stable, so the block is either
+         * unpowered or being silenced by configuration. These are the two writes
+         * the vendor driver performs before it ever starts the CM3, ordered by
+         * how cheap they are to be wrong about. CM3_CFG is re-read after each
+         * one, because a value we did not write is the only evidence the read
+         * path is alive: a register that echoes our own write proves no more
+         * than "something acked the bus". */
+        printf("SWEET GPU: s9 bus/ao start\n");
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 pre CM3_CFG=%#x\n", sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CM3_CFG));
+
+        /* 1. a6xx_gmu_power_config() labels this "disable GMU WB/RB buffer". A GMU
+         * whose bus buffers were never configured can absorb accesses and answer
+         * zero to everything, which is exactly the symptom on the table. */
+        sweet_gpu_wr(gmu, SWEET_GMU_SYS_BUS_CONFIG, 1u);
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c1 SYS_BUS_CONFIG rb=%#x\n",
+            sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_SYS_BUS_CONFIG));
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c1 CM3_CFG=%#x\n", sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CM3_CFG));
+
+        /* 2. a640's own gmu_cgc_mode. If the AO clock controller is parked in a
+         * state the CM3 cannot wake out of, this register records that state and
+         * writing the vendor value is the fix rather than a guess. */
+        sweet_gpu_wr(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CGC_MODE_CNTL, 0x20202u);
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c2 CGC_MODE_CNTL rb=%#x\n",
+            sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CGC_MODE_CNTL));
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c2 CM3_CFG=%#x\n", sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CM3_CFG));
+
+        /* 3. Read-only. A plausible delay/hyst pair with CM3_CFG still zero means
+         * the AO half came up on its own and the CM3 half did not: a split rail,
+         * not a clock gate, and the next move is the rail vote rather than more
+         * register poking. */
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c3 CGC_DELAY_CNTL=%#x\n",
+            sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CGC_DELAY_CNTL));
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c3 CGC_HYST_CNTL=%#x\n",
+            sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CGC_HYST_CNTL));
+
+        /* 4. Sixteen samples, not one (0110 section 9): a live AO block may latch
+         * an interrupt from our own fence and power writes, and a single zero
+         * cannot separate "idle and powered" from "not powered at all". */
+        for (unsigned s = 0; s < 16; ++s) {
+                printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c4 ao_status[%u]=%#x\n", s,
+                    sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_AO_HOST_INTERRUPT_STATUS));
+                delay(1000);
+        }
+        printf("SWEET GMU: S9 c4 ao_mask=%#x\n",
+            sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_AO_HOST_INTERRUPT_MASK));
+
+        /* The verdict this boot exists to produce. Eight more samples so a "still
+         * zero" answer survives the same test that overturned 0108; anything
+         * non-zero here is the CM3 answering for the first time, and the next
+         * boot goes straight to the 0110 section 3 firmware sequence. */
+        for (unsigned s = 0; s < 8; ++s) {
+                printf("SWEET GMU: S9 tail CM3_CFG[%u]=%#x\n", s,
+                    sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CM3_CFG));
+                delay(1000);
+        }
+        printf("SWEET GPU: s9 done alive=%d\n", sweet_gpu_rd(gmu, SWEET_GMU_CM3_CFG) != 0);
 }
 
 #endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_RAPHAEL */
